  You chose local-first (one browser, localStorage), notifications on status/assignment changes, and I proposed
  filtering out self-authored changes. Compose those three and [1mthe feature never fires[0m. In a single-browser app with one
  person, every change is self-authored. The notification system would be complete, correct, and permanently silent.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the local phase produces a second actor.

  - [1mLocal user switcher ("acting as: Alice / Bob"):[0m a dropdown that sets the current actor. Alice assigns a task to Bob,
    switch to Bob, notification is there. Buys a genuinely exercisable feature and a real multi-user data model from
    day one — the actor field is exactly what the server version needs. Costs a small picker UI that gets deleted when
    real auth lands.
  - [1mDon't filter self-changes:[0m every change notifies whoever is subscribed, including you. Zero extra machinery, and the
    feature visibly works immediately. But it's a behavior you will definitely reverse later, and self-notification is
    the single most-complained-about notification bug in real products.
  - [1mAccept a silent local phase:[0m build the store, event log, and inbox UI; verify by test only, with nothing to see in
    the browser until a backend exists. Honest and cheap, but you can't tell whether it works by using it, and it's a
    demo of nothing.

  [1mMy recommendation: the user switcher.[0m It's the only option that makes the local build testable by hand [3mand[0m produces
  the actor model the server version needs unchanged — the picker is throwaway, but the [38;5;153mactor[39m on every event is not.
  Option two saves an afternoon and creates a known-wrong default you'd have to unpick.
